Q: Is there a reliable way to detect if a click had a real effect when app listeners may be attached later? A: <p>There's no good way to handle all click cases... there's just too many potential outcomes depending on the situation. Yes you might detect <em>some</em> action but you still have to validate whether it was the <em>correct</em> action.</p>
<p>What I would suggest is that you use the page object model. Inside of each method that handles an action on the page, the best practice is to wait for the action to succeed/complete before exiting the method. Here's an example method, <code>login()</code>, that handles logging into a website and would be in the <code>LoginPage.java</code> page object.</p>
<pre><code>By usernameLocator = By.id(&quot;username&quot;);
By passwordLocator = By.id(&quot;password&quot;);
By loginButtonLocator = By.cssSelector(&quot;button[name='login']&quot;);

public void login(String username, String password) {
    driver.findElement(usernameLocator).sendKeys(username);
    driver.findElement(passwordLocator).sendKeys(password);
    String url = driver.getCurrentUrl(); // for Option 2
    driver.findElement(loginButtonLocator).click();

    WebDriverWait wait = new WebDriverWait(driver, Duration.ofSeconds(10));
    // Option 1: if the URL target is consistent
    wait.until(ExpectedConditions.urlToBe(&quot;https://desired URL&quot;));

    // Option 2: if the URL target is not consistent
    wait.until(ExpectedConditions.not(ExpectedConditions.urlToBe(url)));
    // or something like
    wait.until(ExpectedConditions.urlMatches(&quot;.*dashboard.*&quot;));

    // Option 3: wait for a portion of the page to reload using stalenessOf() where
    // 'e' is an element in the reloaded portion of the page
    wait.until(ExpectedConditions.stalenessOf(e));
}
</code></pre>
<p>It will take some work, depending on how many pages your tests cover, but once you have this all in place, all clicks will be well handled.</p>

Q: SetValues Formik Function disables Formik HandleSubmit A: <p>Turns out the issue was the is Validating Flag being set to true when you call setFieldValue so the simplest solution is to pass a third argument to setFieldValue to manually turn off validation:</p>

<pre><code>  submit = () =&gt; {
    this.props.setFieldValue('submit', true, false)
    this.props.handleSubmit()
  }
</code></pre>

